@meta
id: 04-05
title: Drill — sixteen down, no scrolling
chapter: 04
type: drill
skills: hjkl, count-prefix
par: 17
minutes: 5
reps: 10
vscode: scroll, squint, click
open: access.log
vimrc: set number

@brief
Ten times: mark the slow request on line 17 with `  SLOW` — two spaces,
then the word — at the end of the line. Save, quit, repeat.

One jump, one count, no arrow keys and no scrolling. If you find yourself
tapping `j` more than once per rep, stop and start the rep again.

@keys
| `16j` | down sixteen lines |
| `A` | append at the end of the line |

@hints
Line 1 to line 17 is sixteen lines of travel.
--
Type the digits before the motion, every time, without checking. The rep
is training the order — number first, motion second.

@solution
    16j A <space><space>SLOW <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The reason this is worth ten repetitions is that the number-then-motion
order has to survive being under pressure. Typed the other way round —
`j16` — you get one line down and then a stray `1` and `6` looking for a
motion of their own, which is harmless but breaks your rhythm.

Counts feel like arithmetic homework for about a week, and then they stop
being numbers at all. You will look at a line four below the cursor and
your hand will type `4j` without any number appearing in your head.

